A||||6||geyser||And yet another Fiero FIRE!!!     MINE!!!||||1||
Z||000000||geyser||03-12-2000||02:52 AM||||While leaving the gas station the other day, just filled up, I noticed from my rearview mirror what looked like steam coming from the engine vents. Since it had just rained and the hood was wet I didn't pay much attention. When the steam would'nt go away I quickly pulled over and opened the engine cover. Damn, my baby was on fire! Grabbed the fire extinguisher I keep in the storage area (I also have one up front) and put out the fire. Further investigation found that the spark plug wires had fallen onto the rear exhaust manifold and caught fire! Minimal damage, thank goodness. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gokart||03-12-2000||06:43 AM||||Thank goodness is right! Good thing you noticed it before it did too much damage. Do you have the plastic spark plug wire holders that keep them in place so they don't fall down by the manifold? Also make sure you route the rear wires in the little space in between the 1st and 2nd rear bolts that hold down the plenum!||||reg||4
Z||000002||William Federle||03-12-2000||08:23 AM||||A similar thing happened to me - after I changed spark plug wires, my car started sputtering one day after leaving a gas station. I pulled over and shut it off and looked in the engine compartment. The wire to the number 1 cyl. (it's an iron duke) had fallen down onto the heat shield and fried.<BR>I got several wire separaters from autozone<BR>(in the HELP) section. The shorter wires now hold the longer ones up off of the shield.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||03-12-2000||09:35 AM||||On a v-6 having the alternator locking up will make your heart skip a beat.  Lots of smoke from that flat belt by the time it is only 1/4 inch wide!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||03-12-2000||04:29 PM||||Your lucky. I always carry an extinguisher with me. A couple of years ago my girl's 84 SE caught fire. I was leaving the MC Donald's drive through and the car stalled. I looked in the rear view and saw smoke coming from the decklid vent(barbecue grill type). I popped the trunk release and saw flames from the wires by the manifold. I poured my soda on it and it saved the car. Come to find out the heat riser tube was touching the wire harness and ignited it. I also found out the car never had the recall done on it. It's done now!||||reg||1
Z||000005||pollock||03-13-2000||02:45 PM||||What was the usual cause of the infamous fiero fires?  Were they typically 6 or 4 cyl cars?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-13-2000||04:00 PM||||There were multiple problems. Wire routing was one. Oil control, especially in the 4 cyl, was another.<P>Even if the recall work was done, you still have to watch wires and leaking valve covers. If you don't, in any car, then a bon fire is a common result.||||reg||1
